I implemented the packet-down frame format from RPRX's design comment in https://github.com/XTLS/Xray-core/pull/6755#issuecomment-5720463978 and opened https://github.com/XTLS/Xray-core/pull/7159.

In short:
- the stream-down response body is cut into packet-down frames with the five elements from the design comment: an omittable session id excerpt, an omittable seq, a mandatory type (data/padding), a mandatory length, and the payload;
- the current use is the one the comment named first: downlink keep-alive. While the downlink has been idle for a full period, the server writes a padding frame instead of staying silent, so the CDN idle timer does not kill the GET while the uplink carries traffic;
- it is opt-in and negotiated per request (the client sends X-Packet-Down on stream-down / stream-one requests, the server confirms in the response header), so servers and clients without the feature are byte-identical to today;
- deliverably out of scope for now, exactly like packet-up today: no ACK, no reordering, no multi-path-down, no retransmission machinery, no obfs. The seq and the excerpt are in the format so these can come later without changing the wire again.

h1 / h2 / h3 are covered, since the framers sit on the response body rather than on one transport. stream-one's single response body is framed the same way.

It does not address the 15-20 KB per TCP connection cut from this issue yet; that needs the bigger packet-down uses later. Testing and review are welcome, especially from people with CDNs in front of the origin, since the target there is exactly the inactivity timer.

